  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1150 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows zonal flow developing at 500 hPa as \nthe ridging in the Four Corners area is dampened. A jet streak \nover the Dakotas is producing some elevated diffluence over this\narea and helping produce some widespread forcing for ascent. At\nthe sfc, a warm front is draped in far northeastern Nebraska. \nThe LLJ is starting to ramp up, too. The VWP at TWX (Topeka) \nshowed wind speeds at 2kft doubling from 8pm to 9pm.\n\nAs the winds picked up and isentropic upglide increased (warm \nair pushed up over the cooler Nebraskan air), we've watched some\nupdrafts develop over the southern tier of counties in the CWA.\nThere is ample instability available (4-5,000 J/kg) and \nmarginally enough shear, but the area that enjoys (?) both shear\nand instability is limited to the small area along the \nNE/KS/MO/IA state lines through the next few hours. The lack of \nshear has already produced some strong but short- lived \nupdrafts. For that reason, severe weather will be hard to \nproduce. Still, large hail up to 1.75\" and perhaps a damaging \nwind gust is possible. The strongest of the convection should be\nout of the CWA by 4am, but isolated PoPs along the KS state \nline could continue as late as noon. \n\nLows will range from upper-60s up north to middle-70s where \nNebraska/Missouri/Kansas meet.",
